A regularized version of the Biot–Savart equation is employed to couple the vorticity in the flow to the velocity field. The vorticity is advected through the three-dimensional (3D) computational space surrounding the rotor using the weighted average-flux scheme of Toro (Ref. 20). This approach explicitly conserves the vorticity as it advects from one computational cell to another and allows the spatial localization of the vortical structures in the wake to be maintained for long computational times. The VTM is thus particularly well suited to computations of the VRS where the flow field is characterized by the long-term persistence of the vorticity distribution near to the rotor.
Simplification of the rotor model
To allow the fundamental kinematical mechanisms underlying the VRS to be identified, the complexity of the system being simulated was reduced to a minimum. First, since practical rotor flows are generally associated with high Reynolds numbers, the simulated flow was assumed to be inviscid by setting ν = 0. Only the vortex kinematics can thus contribute to any dissipation of the wake. Second, the rotor had just one blade to minimize any obscuration of the motion of individual vortex filaments. Third, the flap and lag dynamics of the blade were suppressed so that no perturbations to the geometry or strength of the wake could stem from any irregular motion of its source. Finally, it is believed by the authors that the inherent coupling of the wake dynamics to the blade aerodynamics is an additional, modifying component to the kinematics of the rotor wake during VRS, rather than a fundamental feature of the system that must be present in order for VRS to arise. To expose
